{"answer":"The critic's case rests on a confusion between two distinct notions: 'defenders' in the argumentation-theoretic sense and 'committed defenders' in the procedural sense. The TARGET specifies that the checkpoint verifies 'every committed root is defended by already-committed roots' and suspends 'any root whose only defenders are UNDEC.' An unattacked root has no attackers, so it requires no defenders at all — the condition 'whose only defenders are UNDEC' is vacuously unsatisfied because there are no defenders to evaluate. The grounded extension accepts unattacked nodes precisely because they face no attacks, not because they have defenders. The critic's scenario conflates 'unattacked-and-undefended' (no attackers, no defenders needed) with 'attacked and whose only defenders are UNDEC' (attackers exist but defense is incomplete). The former is accepted by both the grounded semantics and the checkpoint; the latter is correctly suspended. The fixpoint is not altered because the checkpoint only withholds commitment for nodes that genuinely lack grounded defense, which the grounded extension would also leave UNDEC. The claim of a broken guarantee is unsubstantiated: the invariant 'every committed root is valid' is enforced, not violated, since no root is committed unless it is either unattacked or defended by already-committed (and thus grounded-accepted) roots."}